## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a range-based segment cost and path reconstruction, a common advanced pattern not covered yet.

## Key Concepts
- Contiguous partitioning
- Range query (max-min)
- DP with backtracking
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  // base
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let p = i - 1; p >= 0; p--) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[p]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[p]);
      dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i)) - Math.min(...A.slice(0, i));
    }
  }
  // transitions would fill dp[i][t] and cut[i][t]
  return { cost: dp[n][k], partition: cut };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize range(p, i-1) queries to O(1) per transition?
- How would you adapt for 1-indexed arrays or allow at most k segments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests partition DP with a subarray range cost, requiring precomputation of subarray extrema and careful reconstruction. It adds a different objective than prior questions by using (max-min) rather than a function of endpoints or a fixed multiplier. 

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact-k segments
- Subarray range queries (max-min) via precomputation
- Path reconstruction from parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs for small to mid-size n

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  const minv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    maxv[i][i] = A[i];
    minv[i][i] = A[i];
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      maxv[i][j] = Math.max(maxv[i][j-1], A[j]);
      minv[i][j] = Math.min(minv[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = (maxv[0][i-1]-minv[0][i-1]) * i, par[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const cost = (maxv[p][i-1]-minv[p][i-1]) * (i-p);
        if (dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let idx = n, rem = k, cuts = [];
  while (rem > 0) {
    const p = par[idx][rem];
    cuts.push([p, idx-1]);
    idx = p; rem--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks: cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests comprehensive DP design for partitioning problems with nonlinear segment costs involving both min and sum operations, requiring careful handling of base cases and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-dimensional DP over partition indices
- Range minimum query optimization within transitions
- Complete solution from base cases through reconstruction
- Space-time tradeoffs and optimization strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Initialize DP table and parent pointers
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  dp[i][1] = rangeMin(1, i) * prefix[i];
  parent[i][1] = 0;
}

// Fill DP table
for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
  for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
      const segVal = rangeMin(p+1, i) * (prefix[i] - prefix[p]);
      const total = dp[p][t-1] + segVal;
      if (total > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = total;
        parent[i][t] = p;
      }
    }
  }
}

// Reconstruct segments
let i = n, t = k;
while (t > 0) {
  const p = parent[i][t];
  segments[t] = [p+1, i];
  i = p;
  t--;
}
```

